WEBVTT

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.432
Good day, Voyager,

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:08.464
and welcome to
A Briefing with Neelix.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.100
It's a catchy title, isn't it?

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.839
I'll be coming to you
each and every day

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.972
with news of general interest,

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.107
interviews with
our fascinating crew members,

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:19.209
musical performances,

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.743
recommendations
for new holodeck programs

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:25.815
and, of course,
previews of upcoming meals...

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.090
everything from Hlaka soup
to Zabee nuts.

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:33.259
A Briefing with Neelix
will help you stay informed

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.563
about ship's activities,

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:38.196
uh, keep you abreast of
your colleagues' accomplishments

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:41.002
and broaden your knowledge
about a wealth of subjects,

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:45.443
but most of all,
it will make you feel good

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.504
because what you see here
will always be

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.709
the most uplifting,
optimistic view

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.945
of everything
that happens on our ship.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.511
After all,
as your morale officer,

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.785
isn't it my job
to bring a little joy

00:00:57.824 --> 00:01:00.019
into everyone's day, hmm?

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.427
Captain Janeway has agreed
to appear on the show

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.795
with regular updates
about the ship's progress

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.231
and Commander Chakotay

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.701
will commend
outstanding crew members,

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:13.297
so, snap to, and keep those
boots polished to a shine.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.806
You never know
when you might be featured

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:17.401
on A Briefing with Neelix.

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:19.344
Ready, Ensign?

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:20.573
All set, Neelix.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:23.582
In just a moment,
an amazing talent.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.483
Ensign Pablo Baytart
will astound you

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.214
with his juggling ability.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.621
He'll keep padds, hyposprays,
even phasers in the air

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.989
for minutes at a time.

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:35.221
I don't... but before that,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:39.698
what makes people feel better
than a little romance?

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.895
And what Lieutenant
in a gold uniform

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.097
was recently huddled
in the Mess Hall

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.935
with what crewman in blue

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.737
having a very intimate
conversation

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.574
over a carafe of Silmic wine?

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:56.207
Well, I'd never name names,
but you know who you are.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.979
Best wishes
to a handsome pair of lovebirds.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.315
And now,

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:04.153
ladies and gentlemen,
Ensign Pablo Baytart.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.387
Well? What did you think?

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.952
I'm a Doctor, not a performer.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.963
I don't have time
for such nonsense.

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.965
I can see
Hints for Healthful Living

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.830
becoming a very popular
segment, Doctor.

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.393
I don't think so.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.735
You'd be lifted

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.433
to a whole new level
in the crew's eyes.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.409
They'd hang on your every word.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:27.845
You'd enjoy a popularity,
a celebrity

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:30.076
that you've never known before.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.981
I would?

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.578
You don't have
to answer right away.

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:37.988
Just think about it.

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.288
Ah! Mr. Kim!

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.822
Just the person I wanted to see.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:48.993
See you later.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:52.004
Did you watch A Briefing
with Neelix this morning?

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.632
Uh, yes. Yes, I did.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:58.571
Is there something
about the program you disliked?

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:01.808
I'm not sure I care
for all the frosting.

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.645
Frosting?

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.843
Deck 4.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.114
Recipes, music, jugglers...

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.144
it's sort of like
a steady diet of dessert,

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:11.817
which is fine,
but pretty soon, you want

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.587
some meat and potatoes.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.150
You do?

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.560
When I was in school,

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.962
I was editor of the Academy
newspaper for a year.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.966
I monitored subspace
transmissions.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.126
I got reports
on some of the first activity

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.330
by the Maquis
against the Cardassians.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.497
I wrote an editorial

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.565
about it, and the students
became polarized on the issue.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.339
They debated the pros and cons

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.209
and gained an insight
into the entire history

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:36.078
of the political rebellion.

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.912
Now, that's
the power of journalism.

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:41.648
What did your professor say
when you told him

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.212
you were going
to publish the editorial?

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.156
I didn't tell them.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.989
It's the job of a journalist
to be independent.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:51.325
Bridge to Mr. Neelix.

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:53.161
You have
an incoming transmission.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.962
I'm heading for my quarters.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:56.298
Put it through there.

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:57.360
Yes, sir.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:04.506
Neelix, my friend, how are you?

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.944
Laxeth,
you old dilithium scrubber!

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:08.537
It's good to see you.

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:09.946
Calling to trade me

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:11.847
some of your
ill-gotten goods again?

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.215
I promise you
I never want to see

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:16.416
another wheel
of Pendrashian cheese.

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.515
Oh, those days are behind me.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:19.889
I'm legitimate now.

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:23.692
I'm communications master
of a Talaxian convoy.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.925
Communications master...

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.626
well, you've certainly
come up in the world.

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.656
Congratulations.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.760
Looks like I'll be seeing you
in a few days.

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:31.963
How's that?

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.437
We've been asked to rendezvous
with your ship.

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.066
We're going to pick up
a passenger.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:39.840
Passenger?
We don't carry any passengers.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:42.848
Apparently,
it's a member of your crew.

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.510
All I know is
someone wants to leave your ship

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.917
and come to work for us.

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:41.592
Just how, exactly, did you hear

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.602
that someone
was leaving the ship?

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:47.732
An old friend of mine is serving
with a Talaxian convoy.

00:06:47.773 --> 00:06:49.604
He told me they'd be
rendezvousing with us

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:50.836
in a few days.

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:52.138
Janeway to Tuvok.

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:53.145
Yes, Captain?

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:55.170
Could you join us
in my ready room, please?

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:56.203
Right away.

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:04.421
Lieutenant, Mr. Neelix has heard

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:07.392
on good authority
that one of our crew

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:09.860
will be leaving the ship
in a few days.

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.192
Naturally, he's curious

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.289
about the identity
of this person.

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.857
What are the security issues
here?

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:18.734
I don't believe there are
any serious concerns, Captain.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.763
There is no need to withhold
this information any longer.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.943
Lieutenant Paris...

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:28.172
has asked to be
put off the ship.

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.408
Tom?

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:30.415
I'm sure you're aware

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.314
that he's had
some problems lately.

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.046
Well, yes.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.178
He has been disruptive

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:38.088
and often sets a bad example
for the rest of the crew.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:42.652
But... he seemed so determined
to get those things behind him.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:45.926
It means a lot to him
to be respected by this crew.

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:49.298
All I can tell you is that
when he was relieved of duty

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:52.065
he asked me to make arrangements
for him to leave the ship.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.470
I tried to talk him out
of it, but he was adamant.

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:56.498
We detected the Talaxian convoy

00:07:56.542 --> 00:07:59.443
and they were more than happy
to get a pilot of Tom's ability.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.810
I can't believe this.

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:03.906
I never thought...

00:08:04.016 --> 00:08:06.917
Captain, may I speak with him?

00:08:06.952 --> 00:08:08.044
Of course.

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:09.854
He's in his quarters.

00:08:19.463 --> 00:08:20.623
Come in.

00:08:24.702 --> 00:08:26.567
Hello, Tom.

00:08:26.604 --> 00:08:28.663
Neelix.
How's it going?

00:08:34.378 --> 00:08:36.312
Not good, it looks like.

00:08:36.347 --> 00:08:37.575
What's wrong?

00:08:37.615 --> 00:08:40.778
I hear you're leaving the ship.

00:08:43.487 --> 00:08:46.581
Good news travels fast, I guess.

00:08:46.624 --> 00:08:48.387
Yeah, I figured there was

00:08:48.425 --> 00:08:51.519
quite a bit more opportunity
for advancement

00:08:51.562 --> 00:08:53.462
on a Talaxian convoy
than there was

00:08:53.497 --> 00:08:56.125
on the only Starfleet ship
in the Quadrant.

00:08:56.166 --> 00:08:59.465
When did you make this decision?

00:08:59.503 --> 00:09:02.836
Truthfully?
Uh, about a minute and a half

00:09:02.873 --> 00:09:05.467
after I set foot on this ship
a year ago.

00:09:05.509 --> 00:09:08.307
I knew it was a mistake to try
to fit into Starfleet again

00:09:08.345 --> 00:09:10.506
and I should have listened
to myself.

00:09:10.547 --> 00:09:12.538
A few more months
in that rehab colony

00:09:12.583 --> 00:09:15.143
would have been a hell of
a lot better than being here.

00:09:15.185 --> 00:09:18.882
Tom, you and I have
had our differences,

00:09:18.923 --> 00:09:21.255
but I thought we put
those behind us. I...

00:09:21.292 --> 00:09:26.924
I just hope I haven't...
contributed to your decision.

00:09:28.732 --> 00:09:31.030
Neelix, no.

00:09:31.068 --> 00:09:34.037
This isn't about anybody
except me.

00:09:37.007 --> 00:09:40.340
I've done this to myself
just like always.

00:09:40.377 --> 00:09:43.039
No matter where I go
or who I'm with,

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.207
I make a mess of things.

00:09:45.249 --> 00:09:48.946
The unmistakable conclusion
has to be that, deep down,

00:09:49.019 --> 00:09:54.582
I don't want any friends
or a family or a home.

00:09:54.625 --> 00:09:56.388
Otherwise, I wouldn't
keep sabotaging

00:09:56.427 --> 00:09:57.394
the possibilities.

00:09:57.428 --> 00:09:59.726
I thought you found
those things here.

00:09:59.763 --> 00:10:03.324
I tried, or maybe I just
told myself I was trying.

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:04.959
I'm not sure.

00:10:05.069 --> 00:10:07.435
Either way,
it's just not working.

00:10:07.471 --> 00:10:10.406
Frankly, I'd like to get out
before I completely destroy

00:10:10.441 --> 00:10:11.772
my image.

00:10:11.809 --> 00:10:13.401
There might actually be
one or two people

00:10:13.444 --> 00:10:15.605
who still have
a good opinion of me.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.477
I'm one of them, Tom...

00:10:21.151 --> 00:10:23.085
and I wish you wouldn't go.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.987
Well, I thank you.

00:10:26.023 --> 00:10:30.119
But, the truth is, I've always
considered myself a drifter.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.651
You know what that's like,
Neelix.

00:10:32.696 --> 00:10:35.631
Freedom to do what you want,
when you want.

00:10:35.666 --> 00:10:38.794
No rules to follow,
nobody depending on you, right?

00:10:55.219 --> 00:10:57.210
Well, there you are.

00:10:57.254 --> 00:10:59.688
I've been waiting
for what seems like hours.

00:10:59.723 --> 00:11:01.213
Doctor, what can I do for you?

00:11:01.258 --> 00:11:02.885
I want to discuss the details

00:11:02.926 --> 00:11:04.587
of my participation
on your program.

00:11:04.628 --> 00:11:06.118
I've been giving it
a great deal of thought

00:11:06.163 --> 00:11:07.528
and I've come up
with a number of ideas

00:11:07.564 --> 00:11:10.055
I think you'll find
quite intriguing.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.795
Well, that's good news.

00:11:11.835 --> 00:11:14.269
I-I'm delighted you've decided
to become a contributor,

00:11:14.304 --> 00:11:17.273
but I'm afraid I don't have time
to discuss it right now.

00:11:17.307 --> 00:11:18.831
But I've already prepared

00:11:18.876 --> 00:11:21.868
today's topic: "How to keep
your nostrils happy."

00:11:21.912 --> 00:11:23.106
That is fascinating,

00:11:23.147 --> 00:11:26.241
but I'm afraid
there's a more important story

00:11:26.283 --> 00:11:27.716
I have to work on today.

00:11:27.751 --> 00:11:30.777
You mean...
I'm not going to appear today?

00:11:30.821 --> 00:11:35.155
When you see it,
I'm sure you will understand.

00:11:35.192 --> 00:11:37.990
Hmm.
But I will be on tomorrow?

00:11:38.095 --> 00:11:40.928
Definitely.
Without fail, I promise you.

00:11:40.964 --> 00:11:44.365
Very well, but tomorrow,
I'll want to cover two topics.

00:11:44.401 --> 00:11:45.993
I have a most entertaining idea.

00:11:46.103 --> 00:11:49.766
"Uncovering the hidden mysteries
of the adrenal gland."

00:11:49.807 --> 00:11:52.071
Fine, fine.
Good-bye, Doctor.

00:12:06.623 --> 00:12:08.682
Good morning, Voyager.

00:12:08.725 --> 00:12:12.718
I want to tell you
about a friend of mine.

00:12:12.763 --> 00:12:16.927
I first met this man
almost a year ago,

00:12:16.967 --> 00:12:20.300
and, to tell you the truth,
I didn't like him much.

00:12:20.337 --> 00:12:24.899
He seemed a little too cocky,
a little too sure of himself.

00:12:24.942 --> 00:12:27.604
A lot of people had questions
about him.

00:12:29.413 --> 00:12:31.381
He'd proven he'd pretty much
sell himself out

00:12:31.415 --> 00:12:33.815
to the highest bidder,
go wherever the wind blew him.

00:12:33.851 --> 00:12:34.875
So people wondered:

00:12:34.918 --> 00:12:37.716
Could you trust this person
when things got tough?

00:12:37.754 --> 00:12:40.120
Would he stand
side by side with you

00:12:40.157 --> 00:12:44.321
or would he let you down
when you needed him most?

00:12:44.361 --> 00:12:46.158
But the fact of the matter is,

00:12:46.196 --> 00:12:50.997
he proved himself
right from the beginning.

00:12:51.034 --> 00:12:54.834
I wouldn't be alive right now
if it weren't for him,

00:12:54.872 --> 00:12:57.466
and the same goes
for many of you.

00:12:57.508 --> 00:13:00.306
It took me a while
to realize it.

00:13:00.344 --> 00:13:03.245
Like a lot of people,
I was too caught up

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:04.941
in first impressions

00:13:05.015 --> 00:13:07.950
to see the truth
that was right in front of me.

00:13:08.018 --> 00:13:12.250
I overlooked his bravery because
I was focusing on his brashness.

00:13:12.289 --> 00:13:17.955
I ignored his courage
because I saw it as arrogance,

00:13:18.061 --> 00:13:20.188
and I resented his friendliness

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:22.926
because I mistook it
for licentiousness.

00:13:22.966 --> 00:13:26.766
So while this man
was giving us his best

00:13:26.803 --> 00:13:30.899
every minute of every day,
I was busy judging him.

00:13:30.941 --> 00:13:32.932
And now, he's leaving.

00:13:32.976 --> 00:13:35.877
I'm proud to say that in spite
of my narrow-mindedness,

00:13:35.913 --> 00:13:38.780
Thomas Eugene Paris
became my friend.

00:13:38.815 --> 00:13:41.477
I'm going to miss him.

00:13:41.518 --> 00:13:44.282
No more laughs
over a game of pool.

00:13:44.321 --> 00:13:48.485
No more sitting up into
the wee hours swapping stories.

00:13:48.525 --> 00:13:50.493
No more complaints
about my cooking.

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:54.998
Good-bye, Tom.

00:13:55.032 --> 00:13:57.660
I think I speak for more of us
than you might imagine

00:13:57.701 --> 00:13:59.328
when I say
you're going to leave

00:13:59.369 --> 00:14:00.802
an empty space when you go.

00:14:00.837 --> 00:14:03.670
I hope you find
what you're looking for.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.721
Lieutenant, Operations has
detected a minute increase

00:14:26.763 --> 00:14:27.957
in the engine core temperature.

00:14:28.031 --> 00:14:28.963
Are you aware of it?

00:14:29.032 --> 00:14:30.158
Yes, Captain.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.828
There's a slight imbalance
in the magnetic constrictors.

00:14:32.869 --> 00:14:35.133
It's causing the plasma
stream to overheat.

00:14:35.172 --> 00:14:36.400
I have a team working on it.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.805
Keep me posted.

00:14:37.841 --> 00:14:40.810
Mr. Neelix, I'm very
pleased with your program,

00:14:40.844 --> 00:14:43.745
especially your tribute
to Lieutenant Paris.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:45.873
Thank you.

00:14:45.916 --> 00:14:48.248
Captain, have you decided
who's going to replace Paris?

00:14:48.285 --> 00:14:50.253
I have several people in mind.

00:14:50.287 --> 00:14:53.154
Baytart is one.
Hamilton.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.418
Captain, do you think?

00:14:54.458 --> 00:14:59.361
I mean, it's possible Tom might
change his mind, to come back.

00:14:59.396 --> 00:15:01.887
Maybe you shouldn't
replace him so quickly.

00:15:01.932 --> 00:15:05.595
Harry, Lieutenant Paris
made his decision

00:15:05.636 --> 00:15:08.571
with no indication that he
had any doubts about it

00:15:08.605 --> 00:15:11.096
or that he might change
his mind in the near future.

00:15:11.141 --> 00:15:13.302
We have to accept that
and move on.

00:15:13.343 --> 00:15:15.937
What's our tactical status?

00:15:16.046 --> 00:15:17.308
All systems are functional.

00:15:17.347 --> 00:15:18.939
Engineering to Torres.

00:15:19.016 --> 00:15:19.948
Go ahead.

00:15:20.017 --> 00:15:21.450
We have a problem here.

00:15:21.485 --> 00:15:23.316
What is it?

00:15:23.353 --> 00:15:25.378
The magnetic constrictors
have lost alignment.

00:15:25.422 --> 00:15:28.152
The plasma stream
is way too hot.

00:15:28.191 --> 00:15:29.180
It's starting to infect

00:15:29.226 --> 00:15:30.284
the injector valves.

00:15:30.327 --> 00:15:31.294
I'm on my way.

00:15:31.328 --> 00:15:32.818
We're finished here.

00:15:32.863 --> 00:15:34.353
Dismissed.

00:15:34.398 --> 00:15:36.559
Lieutenant, if I may...

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:39.330
this sounds like news
of substance to me.

00:15:41.004 --> 00:15:42.869
Just stay out of our way.

00:15:47.311 --> 00:15:48.278
It's gotten worse.

00:15:48.312 --> 00:15:50.212
I can't get
the injector valves closed.

00:15:50.247 --> 00:15:52.511
Antimatter is starting
to seep into the warp core.

00:15:52.549 --> 00:15:54.949
Have you tried adjusting
the power transfer conduits?

00:15:54.985 --> 00:15:56.350
Yes. No effect.

00:15:56.386 --> 00:15:58.752
We have to try to contain
the reaction in the core.

00:15:58.789 --> 00:16:02.190
Don't let the PTC temperature
get above 3.2 million kelvins.

00:16:02.225 --> 00:16:04.420
It's at 2.9 million now.

00:16:09.132 --> 00:16:11.600
PTC temperature now
at three million kelvins.

00:16:11.635 --> 00:16:13.570
We're going to have
to vent some plasma.

00:16:13.703 --> 00:16:16.729
If you vent plasma that hot
through the nacelles,

00:16:16.773 --> 00:16:18.365
the warp coils are going to fry.

00:16:18.408 --> 00:16:20.239
Better that than a core breach.

00:16:20.276 --> 00:16:22.540
Reinforce the structural field

00:16:22.579 --> 00:16:25.139
around the power
transfer conduits

00:16:25.181 --> 00:16:27.581
and then prepare
to begin venting plasma.

00:16:27.617 --> 00:16:31.451
PTC temperature
now 3.2 million kelvins!

00:16:31.487 --> 00:16:32.511
It's cascading.

00:16:32.555 --> 00:16:34.420
Get away from that console.

00:16:34.457 --> 00:16:35.924
I can stop it from here.

00:16:35.959 --> 00:16:37.654
Get away, Jonas!

00:16:38.828 --> 00:16:40.796
Computer,
initiate plasma venting.

00:16:45.168 --> 00:16:46.260
Are we all right?

00:16:48.371 --> 00:16:49.895
PTC temperature is dropping,

00:16:49.939 --> 00:16:51.270
approaching safe levels.

00:16:52.275 --> 00:16:53.902
We're under control.

00:16:53.943 --> 00:16:54.910
Neelix to Sick Bay.

00:16:54.944 --> 00:16:56.571
Prepare for emergency transport.

00:16:56.613 --> 00:16:58.012
We have three wounded.

00:16:59.682 --> 00:17:01.741
This one's not badly hurt.

00:17:01.784 --> 00:17:03.649
20 milligrams impedrezine.

00:17:03.686 --> 00:17:05.916
Vital signs steady.

00:17:06.022 --> 00:17:08.752
Second degree burns
to face and chest.

00:17:08.791 --> 00:17:12.591
Treat him
with the dermal regenerator.

00:17:12.629 --> 00:17:14.460
Ah. The situation
has been stabilized.

00:17:14.497 --> 00:17:16.692
None of the victims
is in danger any longer.

00:17:16.733 --> 00:17:18.360
May I interview one of them now?

00:17:18.401 --> 00:17:20.392
One of the patients?

00:17:20.436 --> 00:17:21.903
I'd think you'd be
more interested

00:17:21.938 --> 00:17:24.168
in my assessment
of the medical situation.

00:17:24.207 --> 00:17:25.174
Well, I am, of course.

00:17:25.208 --> 00:17:26.539
Would you care
to make a statement?

00:17:26.576 --> 00:17:27.543
Yes.

00:17:27.577 --> 00:17:30.603
The first task is to triage

00:17:30.647 --> 00:17:33.172
or to assess
the relative condition

00:17:33.216 --> 00:17:34.615
of each of the victims

00:17:34.651 --> 00:17:37.449
and then to prioritize
treatment accordingly.

00:17:37.487 --> 00:17:38.818
Thank you, Doctor.

00:17:38.855 --> 00:17:41.153
I'm sure that will be of
great interest to the crew.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:42.748
I wasn't finished.

00:17:42.792 --> 00:17:44.919
Next, the treatment per se
must be supervised.

00:17:44.961 --> 00:17:46.360
Very interesting.

00:17:46.396 --> 00:17:49.661
Now, I know the crew would
love to hear a few words

00:17:49.699 --> 00:17:52.725
from the man who risked his life
to save the ship.

00:17:52.769 --> 00:17:54.396
Would you consent
to an interview?

00:17:54.437 --> 00:17:56.667
I was just doing my job, Neelix.

00:17:56.706 --> 00:17:58.970
Lieutenant Torres is
the one you should talk to.

00:17:59.075 --> 00:18:00.508
Janeway to Neelix.

00:18:00.543 --> 00:18:02.272
Right here, Captain.

00:18:02.311 --> 00:18:04.643
Could you join us
on the Bridge, please?

00:18:04.681 --> 00:18:05.875
I'm on my way.

00:18:05.915 --> 00:18:08.110
I'll contact you later
about the interview.

00:18:12.955 --> 00:18:15.423
The plasma burst irradiated
the engine nacelles.

00:18:15.458 --> 00:18:17.722
The inner layer of the warp
coils was burned away.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.523
Which means
the warp engines are useless

00:18:19.562 --> 00:18:21.291
until we can rebuild them.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:24.493
They're made from a substance
known as verterium cortenide.

00:18:24.534 --> 00:18:26.229
Do you know where we
can find a source?

00:18:26.269 --> 00:18:27.236
Verterium cortenide.

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:28.237
If I'm not mistaken,

00:18:28.271 --> 00:18:31.434
that's a densified
composite material.

00:18:31.474 --> 00:18:32.566
That's right.
It's composed

00:18:32.608 --> 00:18:34.803
of polysilicate verterium
and monocrystal cortenum.

00:18:34.844 --> 00:18:36.471
Do you know
of any nearby source?

00:18:36.512 --> 00:18:39.481
Let's see.

00:18:39.515 --> 00:18:42.609
There's a yellow dwarf
system called Hemikek

00:18:42.652 --> 00:18:46.383
with an M-Class planet
quite rich in minerals.

00:18:46.422 --> 00:18:47.753
Mining rights belong

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.883
to a consortium
of nonaggressive people.

00:18:49.926 --> 00:18:51.791
I'm sure we could
make arrangements

00:18:51.828 --> 00:18:54.194
to purchase
some verterium and cortenum.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:55.458
Good work, Neelix.

00:18:55.498 --> 00:18:59.901
Captain, message coming in
from the Talaxian convoy.

00:18:59.936 --> 00:19:01.335
Put it on screen.

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:03.304
Captain Janeway.

00:19:03.339 --> 00:19:04.306
What's happened?

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.331
We were attacked
by the Kazon-Nistrim.

00:19:06.375 --> 00:19:08.502
Do you have casualties?
How can we help?

00:19:08.544 --> 00:19:10.102
We have no serious casualties

00:19:10.146 --> 00:19:12.171
and they didn't take
any of our cargo.

00:19:12.215 --> 00:19:14.115
The one thing
they were interested in

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:16.584
was your former crewman,
Thomas Paris.

00:19:16.619 --> 00:19:19.417
They took him aboard their ship
and then left.

00:19:19.455 --> 00:19:21.082
I thought you'd want to know.

00:19:44.313 --> 00:19:46.372
Tom Paris...

00:19:46.415 --> 00:19:48.576
Hello, Seska.

00:19:48.618 --> 00:19:50.950
You're looking
radiantly maternal.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:55.150
In another month,
Chakotay will have a son...

00:19:55.191 --> 00:19:57.091
or a daughter.

00:19:57.126 --> 00:19:59.356
I really didn't
want to know which.

00:19:59.395 --> 00:20:01.454
Makes it more interesting,
don't you think?

00:20:01.497 --> 00:20:03.362
I wouldn't know.

00:20:04.801 --> 00:20:07.964
I have no idea which one
Chakotay would prefer.

00:20:08.037 --> 00:20:11.302
He and I rarely see eye to eye.

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.776
You've had your problems
with him lately, too.

00:20:14.811 --> 00:20:16.506
I know all about
what's happened.

00:20:16.546 --> 00:20:19.640
Your sudden inability
to be Starfleet's good boy.

00:20:19.682 --> 00:20:21.206
It's not sudden.

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:23.309
I never really fit in there.

00:20:23.352 --> 00:20:26.810
Then "Lieutenant Tom Paris"
was just a pretense.

00:20:26.856 --> 00:20:28.084
No.

00:20:28.124 --> 00:20:30.592
No, it was me trying very hard

00:20:30.626 --> 00:20:33.060
to be someone
I finally couldn't be.

00:20:34.063 --> 00:20:35.428
I never liked you, Tom.

00:20:37.366 --> 00:20:39.664
I didn't trust you.

00:20:39.702 --> 00:20:42.136
I don't trust you now.

00:20:42.171 --> 00:20:45.766
I can assure you
the feeling is mutual.

00:20:45.808 --> 00:20:47.207
Fine.

00:20:47.243 --> 00:20:50.440
But we might be able
to help each other...

00:20:50.479 --> 00:20:52.538
if you're telling the truth.

00:20:52.582 --> 00:20:55.415
Is that why you kidnapped me
and dragged me here,

00:20:55.451 --> 00:20:57.282
so we can be pals now?

00:20:57.320 --> 00:20:59.550
I thought
it might make sense to you.

00:20:59.589 --> 00:21:00.920
And if it doesn't?

00:21:00.957 --> 00:21:05.860
The Kazon tend to be excessive
in their use of force.

00:21:05.895 --> 00:21:07.726
It's nothing I can change.

00:21:07.763 --> 00:21:10.960
But I can promise you'll be
well-treated on this ship

00:21:11.067 --> 00:21:13.365
if we decide
we can work together.

00:21:13.402 --> 00:21:15.597
What exactly would that mean?

00:21:15.638 --> 00:21:17.162
You're an excellent pilot.

00:21:17.206 --> 00:21:19.265
We could make good use
of your skills,

00:21:19.308 --> 00:21:22.709
and, of course, you have
information about Voyager

00:21:22.745 --> 00:21:24.770
that could be quite valuable.

00:21:24.814 --> 00:21:27.476
It seems to me
like you're already getting

00:21:27.516 --> 00:21:31.077
a lot of valuable information
about Voyager from somewhere.

00:21:33.823 --> 00:21:36.519
Think about it, Tom.

00:21:36.559 --> 00:21:42.225
Think about where you want to be
when the Nistrim seize Voyager

00:21:42.265 --> 00:21:46.668
and start taking control of
the Quadrant sector by sector.

00:21:46.702 --> 00:21:48.431
Just let me know.

00:22:26.509 --> 00:22:30.240
Captain's Log, Stardate 49485.2.

00:22:30.279 --> 00:22:31.371
In spite of the fact

00:22:31.414 --> 00:22:33.814
that Tom Paris has been abducted
by the Kazon,

00:22:33.849 --> 00:22:36.977
we must continue on course
for the Hemikek system.

00:22:37.019 --> 00:22:38.646
Something just doesn't
make sense.

00:22:38.688 --> 00:22:41.418
I mean, he wasn't off the ship
for a day when the Kazon

00:22:41.457 --> 00:22:42.481
attacked that convoy.

00:22:42.525 --> 00:22:44.789
How did they find out so fast
that Tom was there?

00:22:44.827 --> 00:22:46.624
Maybe someone on the convoy
told them.

00:22:46.662 --> 00:22:47.959
A Talaxian convoy

00:22:47.997 --> 00:22:49.931
would never let the Kazon
know where they are.

00:22:49.966 --> 00:22:51.593
That would be like
committing suicide.

00:22:51.634 --> 00:22:53.295
Maybe they came
to rob the cargo,

00:22:53.336 --> 00:22:57.272
but when they realized
there was a Starfleet officer...

00:22:57.306 --> 00:22:59.706
That wouldn't have stopped them
from taking the cargo, too.

00:22:59.742 --> 00:23:01.903
And according to my friend,
Laxeth,

00:23:01.944 --> 00:23:04.037
it was a swift,
efficient strike.

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:07.481
It sounds like they knew
Tom was there

00:23:07.516 --> 00:23:10.178
and they came just to get him.

00:23:10.219 --> 00:23:11.880
But who would have told them?

00:23:12.955 --> 00:23:15.150
One possibility I can think of

00:23:15.191 --> 00:23:17.250
is that it was someone
on this ship.

00:23:17.293 --> 00:23:18.760
On Voyager?

00:23:18.794 --> 00:23:19.954
Who would do that?

00:23:20.062 --> 00:23:22.155
I don't know...

00:23:22.198 --> 00:23:25.634
but a responsible journalist
would find out.

00:23:32.742 --> 00:23:35.677
B'Elanna, I didn't expect
to find you here.

00:23:35.711 --> 00:23:37.110
Are you working the night shift?

00:23:37.146 --> 00:23:39.205
I have been since the accident.

00:23:39.248 --> 00:23:41.273
What brings you here?

00:23:41.317 --> 00:23:43.717
I was wondering
if you could show me

00:23:43.753 --> 00:23:47.086
how to access the subspace
communications logs?

00:23:47.123 --> 00:23:49.614
Why would you be interested
in the com logs?

00:23:49.658 --> 00:23:51.250
Well, just part
of my news program.

00:23:51.293 --> 00:23:53.318
Well, I can get you into them,
but then you're on your own.

00:23:53.362 --> 00:23:54.727
I'm still trying to bring

00:23:54.764 --> 00:23:56.994
the magnetic constrictors
into alignment.

00:23:57.033 --> 00:23:58.762
I understand.
Just get me started.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:06.198
How far back do you want to go?

00:24:06.242 --> 00:24:08.107
Oh, maybe a month or two.

00:24:10.212 --> 00:24:11.647
Okay, the file's open.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:13.509
Transmissions are logged
on by date and time.

00:24:13.548 --> 00:24:14.515
Anything else?

00:24:14.549 --> 00:24:16.847
No, I'll be fine.
Thank you very much.

00:24:16.885 --> 00:24:18.944
Mike, we're going
into the computer core

00:24:18.987 --> 00:24:21.547
to look over the magnetic
constrictor circuitry.

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:23.353
Hold down the fort.
All right?

00:24:23.392 --> 00:24:24.882
Okay, Lieutenant.

00:24:29.164 --> 00:24:31.359
Voyager to Mithren.

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:33.698
Mithren again.

00:24:33.735 --> 00:24:37.296
Voyager to Kotati...

00:24:37.339 --> 00:24:39.364
Are you looking for
something in particular?

00:24:39.408 --> 00:24:43.242
I don't know exactly
what I'm looking for.

00:24:43.278 --> 00:24:45.143
Something anomalous.

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:46.613
Why?

00:24:46.648 --> 00:24:49.014
An incident I'm investigating.

00:24:50.686 --> 00:24:52.153
Tell me, Mr. Hero,

00:24:52.187 --> 00:24:56.647
if someone wanted to make
a secret subspace transmission,

00:24:56.692 --> 00:24:57.681
how would he do it?

00:24:58.694 --> 00:25:00.423
Why would you want
to know that?

00:25:00.462 --> 00:25:03.727
Oh, I can't be too forthcoming
right now,

00:25:03.765 --> 00:25:06.097
but if I find
what I think I'm going to,

00:25:06.134 --> 00:25:10.503
well, let me just say
that this ship will be stunned.

00:25:10.539 --> 00:25:13.702
A secret transmission, huh?

00:25:13.742 --> 00:25:15.539
Well, that would be tough,
Neelix.

00:25:15.577 --> 00:25:18.205
There are all kinds of
security routines in place

00:25:18.246 --> 00:25:21.238
and any time a subspace
antenna array is in use,

00:25:21.283 --> 00:25:24.047
the sensors make a record of it.

00:25:24.086 --> 00:25:26.384
I'm not seeing anything
anomalous here.

00:25:26.421 --> 00:25:27.945
What about that?

00:25:27.989 --> 00:25:28.956
What?

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:30.389
That... gap.

00:25:30.425 --> 00:25:34.191
As if some of the log entries
have been deleted.

00:25:34.229 --> 00:25:35.958
Well, that's just
a data fragmentation.

00:25:36.031 --> 00:25:37.157
I beg your pardon?

00:25:37.199 --> 00:25:38.166
It probably happened

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.827
when the plasma
overloaded the system.

00:25:39.868 --> 00:25:40.892
Little glitches like this

00:25:40.936 --> 00:25:42.733
will be popping up for
weeks, I'd imagine.

00:25:42.771 --> 00:25:44.204
Here it is again.

00:25:44.239 --> 00:25:45.501
I wouldn't worry about it.

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:48.168
Maybe Lieutenant Torres
can explain it.

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:50.371
There's nothing to explain.

00:25:50.412 --> 00:25:54.075
Believe me, Neelix,
you're making too much of it.

00:25:54.116 --> 00:25:56.983
Ah! Here it is again.

00:25:57.018 --> 00:25:58.747
Gaps in the log entries.

00:25:58.787 --> 00:26:02.382
And, frankly, it looks like more
than data fragmentation to me.

00:26:05.193 --> 00:26:10.927
The gaps are showing up in logs
entered over the last month

00:26:10.966 --> 00:26:13.400
but not before that.

00:26:13.435 --> 00:26:14.925
So, there you are!

00:26:15.036 --> 00:26:16.833
I've been looking for you,
Mr. Neelix.

00:26:16.872 --> 00:26:18.931
Doctor, how can I help you?

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:21.736
You again neglected to include
me in your news program.

00:26:21.777 --> 00:26:23.904
If you remember, I was
to have two segments today

00:26:23.945 --> 00:26:25.378
because I was excluded
yesterday.

00:26:25.413 --> 00:26:27.244
That's right, that's right.

00:26:27.282 --> 00:26:29.944
I've been so preoccupied
with this other matter.

00:26:30.051 --> 00:26:31.109
In the interest of time,

00:26:31.153 --> 00:26:33.121
I'm willing to forego
my story on the adrenal gland.

00:26:33.155 --> 00:26:35.646
I will concentrate instead
on my latest idea

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.955
"The Klingon Glottis:
Friend or Foe?"

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:40.584
That's very interesting,

00:26:40.629 --> 00:26:42.529
but we're going to have
to put it off until tomorrow.

00:26:42.564 --> 00:26:44.122
That's what you said yesterday.

00:26:44.166 --> 00:26:46.691
I'm sorry, Doctor,
but I really have to go now.

00:26:57.078 --> 00:26:58.443
All through

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:02.177
the subspace communication logs,
there were these gaps

00:27:02.217 --> 00:27:04.515
as though something
had been erased.

00:27:04.553 --> 00:27:07.021
We have recently experienced
a plasma overload.

00:27:07.055 --> 00:27:08.352
That could easily account

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:10.449
for the anomalous readings
in the logs.

00:27:10.492 --> 00:27:12.653
Aha! I knew you'd say that.

00:27:12.694 --> 00:27:14.525
Answer this, Mr. Vulcan:

00:27:14.563 --> 00:27:17.464
Why would those gaps,
those deletions

00:27:17.499 --> 00:27:19.729
occur only in the sub com logs

00:27:19.768 --> 00:27:21.736
and only in the last month?

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:23.931
I checked
every log system we have...

00:27:23.972 --> 00:27:26.634
sensor logs,
power allocation logs,

00:27:26.675 --> 00:27:29.235
warp maintenance logs,
environmental control logs.

00:27:29.277 --> 00:27:30.539
They're all perfectly fine.

00:27:33.648 --> 00:27:34.876
What are you suggesting?

00:27:34.916 --> 00:27:37.783
I believe someone on this ship

00:27:37.819 --> 00:27:40.287
has been making
covert transmissions

00:27:40.322 --> 00:27:42.119
and erasing the evidence.

00:27:42.157 --> 00:27:43.556
To what end?

00:27:43.592 --> 00:27:47.289
I don't know,
but I suspect whoever it was

00:27:47.329 --> 00:27:49.593
has been communicating
with the Kazon.

00:27:49.631 --> 00:27:52.259
That's
an extremely serious charge.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:53.790
I know.

00:27:53.835 --> 00:27:55.564
That's why I've come to you.

00:27:57.939 --> 00:28:02.069
Mr. Neelix, I strongly suspect
that your enthusiasm

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.340
for your new
journalistic endeavor

00:28:04.379 --> 00:28:07.507
is leading you to see villains
where none exist.

00:28:07.549 --> 00:28:10.746
However, even the possibility
that someone on board

00:28:10.785 --> 00:28:12.912
might secretly be communicating
with the Kazon

00:28:12.988 --> 00:28:14.649
deserves investigation.

00:28:14.689 --> 00:28:16.680
I will pursue the matter.

00:28:16.725 --> 00:28:18.283
And I will help you.

00:28:18.326 --> 00:28:19.554
I think not.

00:28:19.594 --> 00:28:21.858
This is now a matter
of ship security.

00:28:21.897 --> 00:28:24.525
I must ask you to withdraw
and let me handle it

00:28:24.566 --> 00:28:26.158
according
to Starfleet protocols.

00:28:26.201 --> 00:28:29.659
As a journalist, I have
an ethical obligation

00:28:29.704 --> 00:28:32.434
to keep digging
for the facts in this case.

00:28:32.474 --> 00:28:34.066
And I have the higher obligation

00:28:34.109 --> 00:28:36.942
to keep you from putting
yourself and others at risk.

00:28:36.978 --> 00:28:38.639
You will drop this matter.

00:28:42.284 --> 00:28:44.411
I see.

00:28:44.452 --> 00:28:46.886
All right, I leave it
in your hands.

00:28:51.159 --> 00:28:52.524
Good hunting.

00:28:54.296 --> 00:28:56.821
"A higher obligation"?

00:28:56.865 --> 00:28:59.459
Ha. I'll show him
higher obligation.

00:28:59.501 --> 00:29:02.265
He'll have Eskarian egg
on his face

00:29:02.304 --> 00:29:04.135
when I get to the heart of this.

00:29:04.172 --> 00:29:07.232
It's the job of a journalist
to be independent.

00:29:09.244 --> 00:29:11.769
Lieutenant, I wonder
if you could...

00:29:11.813 --> 00:29:13.212
I'm a little busy right now,
Neelix.

00:29:13.248 --> 00:29:14.442
What do you need?

00:29:14.482 --> 00:29:15.881
I was wondering

00:29:15.917 --> 00:29:18.385
if it's possible
to look underneath a log

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.354
that seems to have been erased,

00:29:20.388 --> 00:29:22.379
see if there's
anything left of it.

00:29:22.424 --> 00:29:26.121
Mr. Hogan, could you please
help Neelix out for a minute?

00:29:26.161 --> 00:29:28.857
You want to recover
deleted logs?

00:29:28.897 --> 00:29:30.592
Uh, that would be ideal.

00:29:30.632 --> 00:29:33.157
Well, let me give it a try.

00:29:33.201 --> 00:29:35.192
Usually it's possible
to recover data

00:29:35.236 --> 00:29:36.464
unless extraordinary
measures

00:29:36.504 --> 00:29:38.131
were taken to erase it.

00:29:41.142 --> 00:29:43.303
Well, this is strange.

00:29:43.345 --> 00:29:45.939
Computer, run a signal
modulation analysis

00:29:46.014 --> 00:29:47.311
of this file.

00:29:47.349 --> 00:29:49.715
Security authorization required.

00:29:49.751 --> 00:29:52.276
Engineering authorization:
Omega-four-seven.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:54.686
Authorization accepted.

00:29:54.723 --> 00:29:56.418
Running analysis.

00:29:58.360 --> 00:29:59.452
What?

00:29:59.494 --> 00:30:02.190
I... can't recover the logs

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:04.130
because these messages were sent

00:30:04.165 --> 00:30:06.497
without using
any of the antenna arrays.

00:30:06.534 --> 00:30:08.126
How could someone do that?

00:30:08.169 --> 00:30:11.866
By sending the transmissions
through the ship's power grid.

00:30:11.906 --> 00:30:13.237
The messages were encoded

00:30:13.274 --> 00:30:15.834
in the waste energy
from the propulsion systems,

00:30:15.877 --> 00:30:17.970
which is
almost indistinguishable

00:30:18.046 --> 00:30:19.741
from galactic background noise.

00:30:19.781 --> 00:30:21.612
Ingenious.

00:30:21.649 --> 00:30:23.549
Would there be a way

00:30:23.585 --> 00:30:26.850
to determine
who sent the messages?

00:30:26.888 --> 00:30:30.324
I'm looking
for signal correlation traces.

00:30:30.358 --> 00:30:33.122
They'd indicate a com link
to the EPS conduits...

00:30:33.161 --> 00:30:35.220
maybe give us a location.

00:30:35.263 --> 00:30:38.232
I don't know if I can...

00:30:38.266 --> 00:30:39.426
There.

00:30:39.467 --> 00:30:40.900
There's one.

00:30:43.271 --> 00:30:48.766
The source of the com link
is on Deck 4, Section 3-C.

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:51.278
Sorry, but I can't get
more specific than that.

00:30:51.312 --> 00:30:53.109
Mr. Hogan, I need
your help over here.

00:30:53.148 --> 00:30:55.013
Thanks for your help.

00:30:55.083 --> 00:30:57.108
I'll check out Deck 4.

00:30:57.152 --> 00:30:59.347
Go room by room,
console by console.

00:30:59.387 --> 00:31:01.787
I'd look for evidence
of recent deletions.

00:31:01.823 --> 00:31:04.291
If you see any,
run a recovery program.

00:31:04.325 --> 00:31:05.417
See what you get.

00:31:24.846 --> 00:31:26.973
Computer, activate monitor.

00:31:27.048 --> 00:31:29.278
Unable to comply.

00:31:29.317 --> 00:31:32.775
Monitor is cleared for
Lieutenant Thomas Paris only.

00:31:32.821 --> 00:31:34.516
Override existing clearance.

00:31:34.556 --> 00:31:37.787
Engineering authorization:
Omega-four-seven.

00:31:37.826 --> 00:31:39.885
Authorization accepted.

00:31:39.928 --> 00:31:41.919
Files recovered.

00:31:54.576 --> 00:31:56.737
Unbelievable.

00:31:58.079 --> 00:32:01.412
My friends,
I derive no satisfaction

00:32:01.449 --> 00:32:03.349
from what I am
about to tell you.

00:32:03.384 --> 00:32:05.944
I know I originally promised you

00:32:05.987 --> 00:32:08.251
that I would only bring you
good news.

00:32:08.289 --> 00:32:12.953
However, I feel it's important
that you know the truth.

00:32:13.061 --> 00:32:17.498
I have uncovered evidence
that casts serious doubt

00:32:17.532 --> 00:32:20.524
on the loyalties
of Lieutenant Thomas Paris.

00:32:20.568 --> 00:32:23.628
I have discovered secret
communications to the Kazon

00:32:23.671 --> 00:32:25.298
that were sent from his quarters

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:27.240
just prior
to his leaving the ship.

00:32:27.275 --> 00:32:28.674
Get him up here... now.

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:30.803
...of messages sent to the Kazon

00:32:30.845 --> 00:32:33.280
for some weeks
before his departure.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:39.976
How did you trace those
transmissions to Tom's quarters?

00:32:40.053 --> 00:32:41.987
It was, uh...

00:32:42.055 --> 00:32:45.422
I believe it was called
signal correlation traces.

00:32:45.458 --> 00:32:48.950
Mr. Hogan was helping me,
and he detected them.

00:32:48.995 --> 00:32:50.826
When I looked for signal
correlation traces,

00:32:50.864 --> 00:32:52.229
there were none.

00:32:52.265 --> 00:32:54.233
That means someone placed
them in the system

00:32:54.267 --> 00:32:55.996
after my initial investigation.

00:32:56.069 --> 00:32:58.367
What initial investigation?

00:32:58.405 --> 00:33:00.430
Captain, what's going on?

00:33:04.277 --> 00:33:08.043
What I'm about to say will come
as a surprise to you, Commander.

00:33:08.081 --> 00:33:10.948
There has been a spy
aboard Voyager,

00:33:10.984 --> 00:33:13.953
but it isn't Tom Paris.

00:33:13.987 --> 00:33:15.955
It isn't?

00:33:16.056 --> 00:33:18.718
No. Lieutenant Paris is,
in fact, part of our plan.

00:33:18.758 --> 00:33:20.885
Plan?

00:33:20.927 --> 00:33:22.485
Some weeks ago, Tuvok
discovered evidence

00:33:22.529 --> 00:33:24.053
of covert transmissions

00:33:24.097 --> 00:33:26.691
leaving Voyager
through the EPS system.

00:33:26.733 --> 00:33:28.564
We realized there was
someone on this ship

00:33:28.601 --> 00:33:30.296
communicating
with the Kazon-Nistrim.

00:33:30.337 --> 00:33:31.429
However,

00:33:31.471 --> 00:33:34.372
we were unable to ascertain
who that person was.

00:33:34.407 --> 00:33:36.170
We needed a way
to flush out the spy

00:33:36.209 --> 00:33:38.404
and we had exhausted
all conventional means.

00:33:38.445 --> 00:33:40.606
That's when
we thought of using Tom,

00:33:40.647 --> 00:33:43.309
finding a way to get him
on the Kazon ship.

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:46.911
Are you saying Paris'
insulting behavior,

00:33:46.953 --> 00:33:49.080
the gambling,
being late for duty,

00:33:49.122 --> 00:33:51.920
mouthing off at me,
was all a ruse?

00:33:51.958 --> 00:33:53.448
That is correct.

00:33:53.493 --> 00:33:55.256
It was important that his
decision to leave the ship

00:33:55.295 --> 00:33:59.163
appear plausible, so he began
behaving like a malcontent.

00:33:59.199 --> 00:34:01.929
And the reason I wasn't let in
on this little plan?

00:34:02.001 --> 00:34:03.935
I was the one who recommended

00:34:04.003 --> 00:34:06.164
to Captain Janeway
that you not be told.

00:34:06.206 --> 00:34:08.572
I suspected that the spy
was a Maquis

00:34:08.608 --> 00:34:11.270
and felt it was wrong
to put you in a position

00:34:11.311 --> 00:34:13.836
of setting a trap for someone
who had once served under you.

00:34:13.880 --> 00:34:15.643
In other words,
you didn't trust me.

00:34:15.682 --> 00:34:21.120
Commander, the simple fact is
we needed a good performance.

00:34:21.154 --> 00:34:24.123
I'm afraid we used you
to help Tom provide it,

00:34:24.157 --> 00:34:26.591
and you did a damn good job.

00:34:29.629 --> 00:34:31.790
Now, it seems Mr. Neelix's
investigation

00:34:31.831 --> 00:34:33.924
has made someone nervous...

00:34:33.967 --> 00:34:35.628
nervous enough to put a trail

00:34:35.668 --> 00:34:38.068
in the computer system
for him to follow.

00:34:38.104 --> 00:34:39.469
Could it be Hogan?

00:34:39.506 --> 00:34:41.303
I don't think so.

00:34:41.341 --> 00:34:43.935
He was just the person
walking by

00:34:43.977 --> 00:34:46.571
who Lieutenant Torres
asked to help me.

00:34:46.613 --> 00:34:48.843
The spy may be
a little cozy now,

00:34:48.882 --> 00:34:51.976
since you took the bait
and pointed the finger at Tom,

00:34:52.051 --> 00:34:54.110
but we don't want him cozy.

00:34:54.154 --> 00:34:55.917
I'd rather he feel
some pressure.

00:34:55.955 --> 00:34:57.217
Perhaps we should
take advantage

00:34:57.257 --> 00:34:59.748
of Mr. Neelix's
unintentional blunder.

00:34:59.793 --> 00:35:02.921
Exactly. I want you
to keep investigating, Neelix.

00:35:02.962 --> 00:35:05.055
Do what you've been doing...
poke around, ask questions...

00:35:05.098 --> 00:35:07.123
but let people think

00:35:07.167 --> 00:35:10.364
you're not so sure
you were right about Tom Paris.

00:35:10.403 --> 00:35:13.395
That would put Neelix in a
potentially dangerous situation.

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:16.170
We would keep an open com link
to you at all hours.

00:35:16.209 --> 00:35:18.268
My security team
could act immediately

00:35:18.311 --> 00:35:20.939
if anything were to happen.

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:22.413
It's up to you, Neelix.

00:35:22.449 --> 00:35:24.076
This is purely voluntary.

00:35:31.057 --> 00:35:34.151
Anything I can do to help,
Captain,

00:35:34.194 --> 00:35:38.324
and get Tom home safely,
I want to do.

00:35:58.852 --> 00:36:00.410
What did you expect?

00:36:00.453 --> 00:36:04.389
...think this has all been about
exchanging holiday greetings?

00:36:04.424 --> 00:36:06.255
No, of course not, but...

00:36:06.292 --> 00:36:07.691
Here's how it's going to work.

00:36:07.727 --> 00:36:10.696
We'll be waiting for you
on a planet

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:11.924
called Hemikek IV.

00:36:14.934 --> 00:36:17.027
Well, Tom, it's always good

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:19.038
to have one's
instincts verified.

00:36:19.072 --> 00:36:22.439
Clever plan, Seska,
leading Voyager into an ambush,

00:36:22.475 --> 00:36:26.275
but even so, this ship
will be outmanned and outgunned.

00:36:26.312 --> 00:36:28.803
That's why we have
ground troops on Hemikek

00:36:28.848 --> 00:36:30.543
and more ships on the way.

00:36:30.583 --> 00:36:33.143
Now, don't you wish
you'd taken my offer?

00:36:52.939 --> 00:36:54.873
Thanks for the hospitality.

00:36:55.909 --> 00:36:57.240
Go get him!

00:37:58.538 --> 00:38:00.938
Lieutenant Torres is waiting
for these interlink sequencers.

00:38:00.974 --> 00:38:02.066
Get them to her right away.

00:38:02.108 --> 00:38:03.075
Yes, sir.

00:38:03.109 --> 00:38:04.076
Excuse me.

00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:05.077
Oh, hello, Neelix.

00:38:05.111 --> 00:38:06.806
I was wondering if one
of you could help me.

00:38:06.846 --> 00:38:08.336
We're a little busy right now.

00:38:08.381 --> 00:38:09.609
What is it that you need?

00:38:09.649 --> 00:38:12.914
The signal correlation traces
that implicated Tom Paris...

00:38:13.019 --> 00:38:15.613
they were added
to the file after the fact.

00:38:15.655 --> 00:38:16.815
You're kidding?

00:38:16.856 --> 00:38:20.155
So, I was trying to figure out
how that could have happened

00:38:20.193 --> 00:38:21.558
and who might have done it.

00:38:21.594 --> 00:38:23.118
This couldn't be
a worse time, Neelix.

00:38:23.162 --> 00:38:25.756
We're getting everything ready
for rebuilding the warp coils.

00:38:25.798 --> 00:38:27.425
Do you mind if I use

00:38:27.467 --> 00:38:29.662
the diagnostic terminal
over there?

00:38:29.702 --> 00:38:32.865
Go ahead, just don't tap
into any Engineering systems.

00:38:32.905 --> 00:38:34.202
I'll be careful.

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:35.207
Torres to Engineering.

00:38:35.241 --> 00:38:36.765
Jonas here, Lieutenant.

00:38:36.809 --> 00:38:39.300
I need the electrodynamic
load specs.

00:38:39.345 --> 00:38:40.903
They weren't with
the other things you sent.

00:38:40.947 --> 00:38:41.914
Sorry, Lieutenant.

00:38:41.948 --> 00:38:43.074
I'll get them to you right now.

00:38:43.116 --> 00:38:44.310
Take these to Lieutenant Torres.

00:38:44.350 --> 00:38:45.908
Right.

00:38:52.091 --> 00:38:53.149
What's going on?

00:38:53.192 --> 00:38:54.659
Bridge to Engineering.

00:38:54.694 --> 00:38:56.423
Jonas here, Captain.

00:38:56.462 --> 00:38:58.123
I need you to increase
transporter range.

00:38:58.164 --> 00:39:00.598
Shunt all available power
to Transporter Room 2.

00:39:00.633 --> 00:39:01.600
Right away.

00:39:01.634 --> 00:39:03.101
Let me know as soon
as you've done it.

00:39:03.136 --> 00:39:04.160
We have an incoming shuttle

00:39:04.203 --> 00:39:05.693
and we've identified
human life signs.

00:39:05.738 --> 00:39:07.706
It may be Tom Paris.

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:10.038
Yes, ma'am.

00:39:15.415 --> 00:39:18.441
Paris to Voyager!
Paris to Voyager!

00:39:18.484 --> 00:39:20.145
Do you read me yet?

00:39:22.455 --> 00:39:23.615
Paris to Voyager!

00:39:23.656 --> 00:39:26.420
Paris to Voyager!
Come in!

00:39:26.459 --> 00:39:28.120
Voyager to Paris.
We hear you.

00:39:28.161 --> 00:39:30.493
Captain, change your course
immediately.

00:39:30.530 --> 00:39:32.555
The Kazon have ground troops
waiting for you

00:39:32.598 --> 00:39:35.431
on Hemikek,
and more ships on the way.

00:39:35.468 --> 00:39:37.197
We'll get you out of there
as soon as we can, Tom.

00:39:37.236 --> 00:39:38.635
You're still
out of transporter range,

00:39:38.671 --> 00:39:40.935
but we won't change course until
we've locked on to you.

00:39:41.007 --> 00:39:42.497
I can take care of myself!

00:39:42.542 --> 00:39:44.134
Just get Voyager to safety!

00:39:44.177 --> 00:39:46.168
As soon as you're on board.

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:49.114
Tom, what happened?
Are you all right?

00:39:49.148 --> 00:39:51.207
Lieutenant Paris, report.

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:54.378
Mr. Jonas,
we need that transporter now.

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:55.387
I'm working on it, Captain.

00:39:55.421 --> 00:39:58.151
I'm having trouble resetting
the EPS manifold.

00:39:58.191 --> 00:39:59.180
Stay on it.

00:40:01.561 --> 00:40:03.222
Mr. Jonas, I'm no expert,

00:40:03.262 --> 00:40:04.854
but it doesn't look to me
like you're working

00:40:04.897 --> 00:40:06.797
on the transporter subsystems.

00:40:06.833 --> 00:40:08.733
Neelix, I know what I'm doing.

00:40:15.174 --> 00:40:16.402
What did you do?!

00:40:19.245 --> 00:40:20.974
What is this force field for?

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:22.479
It's just a precaution.

00:40:22.515 --> 00:40:23.982
Please, Neelix!

00:40:24.016 --> 00:40:25.244
Stay out of this!

00:40:28.754 --> 00:40:30.984
Tom, can you hear me?
Report.

00:40:31.056 --> 00:40:32.819
I'm here, Captain.

00:40:32.858 --> 00:40:35.418
We'll be in normal transporter
range in another minute.

00:40:37.029 --> 00:40:38.894
Captain... the spy.

00:40:40.032 --> 00:40:41.693
It's Michael Jonas.

00:40:41.734 --> 00:40:45.170
He's the one
that sabotaged the ship.

00:40:45.204 --> 00:40:47.434
Noted.
Janeway to Tuvok.

00:40:47.473 --> 00:40:49.134
Get to Engineering immediately.

00:40:49.174 --> 00:40:50.766
It's Michael Jonas.

00:40:50.809 --> 00:40:52.003
On our way.

00:40:52.044 --> 00:40:55.377
Shuttle hull starting to breach.

00:40:55.414 --> 00:40:57.348
Might not make it, Captain.

00:40:59.318 --> 00:41:01.252
I recognize that subroutine.

00:41:01.286 --> 00:41:02.878
You're into the weapons system.

00:41:02.921 --> 00:41:04.115
You're disabling them!

00:41:04.156 --> 00:41:06.454
For God's sakes,
Neelix, back off.

00:41:06.492 --> 00:41:07.459
Neelix to...

00:41:28.614 --> 00:41:30.445
We're in transporter range,
Captain.

00:41:30.482 --> 00:41:32.074
Voyager to Paris.

00:41:32.117 --> 00:41:33.550
Tom, can you hear me?

00:41:33.585 --> 00:41:35.075
We're energizing now.

00:41:39.425 --> 00:41:41.586
Transporter Room 2,
do you have him?

00:41:41.627 --> 00:41:43.686
We have him, Captain.
He's all right.

00:41:46.298 --> 00:41:47.265
It's the Kazon ship, Captain.

00:41:47.299 --> 00:41:48.630
Return fire.

00:41:48.667 --> 00:41:50.999
Janeway to Tuvok,
are you in Engineering yet?

00:41:51.103 --> 00:41:52.468
The doors are sealed.

00:41:52.504 --> 00:41:53.835
We're working on it, Captain.

00:41:53.872 --> 00:41:55.305
Bridge to Transporter Room 2.

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:57.001
Lock on to Mr. Jonas
in Engineering

00:41:57.076 --> 00:41:58.338
and beam him out of there.

00:41:58.377 --> 00:41:59.639
Aye, Captain.

00:42:06.919 --> 00:42:09.444
All I got was his combadge,
Captain.

00:42:11.657 --> 00:42:13.147
Captain, we've lost weapons.

00:42:13.192 --> 00:42:15.717
It looks like the phaser
arrays have been disabled

00:42:15.761 --> 00:42:16.887
from Engineering.

00:42:16.929 --> 00:42:18.453
Evasive maneuvers, Ensign.

00:42:18.497 --> 00:42:20.863
Mr. Kim, get those weapons
back on line.

00:43:21.493 --> 00:43:23.120
Weapons are back on line,
Captain.

00:43:23.162 --> 00:43:24.493
Fire at will.

00:43:24.530 --> 00:43:26.395
Yes, ma'am.

00:43:39.945 --> 00:43:41.537
Are you injured?

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:42.979
Don't worry about me,
Mr. Vulcan.

00:43:43.048 --> 00:43:44.447
I'm perfectly all right.

00:43:44.483 --> 00:43:47.281
Unfortunately, Mr. Jonas
wasn't so lucky.

00:43:47.319 --> 00:43:49.287
Bridge to Tuvok.

00:43:49.321 --> 00:43:51.482
I'm standing down Red Alert.

00:43:51.523 --> 00:43:55.118
Well, I'm going to have
plenty of material

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:58.357
for tomorrow's
Briefing with Neelix.

00:43:58.397 --> 00:44:00.422
...and fortunately,
I was able to steal

00:44:00.465 --> 00:44:02.592
one of the Kazon shuttles
and escape.

00:44:02.634 --> 00:44:06.035
An amazing story.
Amazing.

00:44:06.071 --> 00:44:08.437
Now, I'd like to say
something more, if I might.

00:44:08.473 --> 00:44:10.407
Of course.

00:44:10.442 --> 00:44:13.536
Um, I know that I've
been acting like a jerk

00:44:13.579 --> 00:44:15.740
for the last couple of months.

00:44:15.781 --> 00:44:18.045
Unfortunately,
I had to behave that way

00:44:18.083 --> 00:44:19.345
if the spy was going to believe

00:44:19.384 --> 00:44:21.409
that I really wanted
to leave the ship,

00:44:21.453 --> 00:44:24.422
so I'd like to apologize
to anyone

00:44:24.456 --> 00:44:26.754
that I might have offended...

00:44:26.792 --> 00:44:29.727
uh, especially
Commander Chakotay.

00:44:29.761 --> 00:44:32.161
I gave him a pretty hard time.

00:44:32.197 --> 00:44:34.722
Not that it wasn't a certain
amount of fun, mind you.

00:44:36.368 --> 00:44:39.531
Well, there you have it,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:44:39.571 --> 00:44:41.664
the true hero
of this whole endeavor...

00:44:41.707 --> 00:44:44.141
Lieutenant Thomas Eugene Paris.

00:44:44.176 --> 00:44:46.906
Well, I'm afraid
that's all the time we have.

00:44:46.945 --> 00:44:48.970
Oh, I'd like to apologize
to the Doctor

00:44:49.014 --> 00:44:52.074
for not getting around
to him once again today,

00:44:52.117 --> 00:44:54.415
but I promise you,
he'll be here tomorrow

00:44:54.453 --> 00:44:56.512
with lots of fascinating
information

00:44:56.555 --> 00:44:58.352
about the Bolian
digestive system.

00:44:58.390 --> 00:44:59.823
Good day, Voyager.
